{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>How shall I curse whom Elohim has not cursed, etc. For from the beginning of the rocks, etc.</b> It is difficult: if HaShem has cursed this people, what place is there for Balaam to curse? And if HaShem is wrathful with them, what need is there for Balaam's wrath? Surely Balak called him only to curse one who is not cursed, etc. Further, one must understand what logical connection the statement <b>for from the beginning of the rocks</b> etc. has to the statement <b>how shall I curse</b> etc." "To grasp the intent of the words, it is essential to first note the fundamental principle concerning a man who is cursed. Whichever way you look at it: if he is guilty according to the law, the evil that comes upon him through the instrumentality of the curse would befall him in any event even if he were not cursed; and if according to the judgment of Elohim he has done nothing to obligate the onset of evil, his curse returns upon the head of the one who curses. This is fine in the case of blessing, for since the measure of good is abundantly multiplied — and also to fulfill HaShem's desire to show lovingkindness to His beloved by blessing those who receive blessing from them — his blessing is accepted even for one who is not worthy of it according to his deeds, which is not so with the measure of evil, for HaShem does not bring evil upon a person for nothing, heaven forbid." "<b>But</b> the essential principle is that His attribute, may He be blessed, is to be slow to anger toward those who transgress His will — whether a righteous person when he sins, or a wicked person even when he merely intends to sin — and this attribute is one of the thirteen attributes of mercy that were stated to Moses and operate continually, except at the time when HaShem is wrathful, as it is written: ״כִּי רֶגַע בְּאַפּוֹ״ (Psalms 30:6). At that time the judgments prevail over the one demanded in judgment at that moment, and this attribute of patient forbearance does not govern. And likewise, when a person curses his fellow, the curse causes the sins for which HaShem has been prolonging His anger against him to be swiftly exacted from him. But if he has no sin, the curse of the one who curses avails nothing at all." "<b>Further,</b> I will give you insight: every doer of evil produces a blemish in the supernal attribute, in that branch which is dependent upon the commandment he has transgressed. And he does not blemish the entire attribute, but only at the root of his soul. The blemish is that it creates a foothold for the distant portion to stand there and draw nourishment from the source of life. And this distant portion is embedded — its end — within the sinner, and its head draws sustenance from the place of the root of the sinning soul. And this endures as long as the person has not repented or has not been punished for his sin. And this is the sod of ״נוֹשֵׂא עָוֹן״ that our Rabbis of blessed memory said — that HaShem sustains the prosecuting adversary created from a person's sin, for it is this adversary itself that is exacted from the person himself (Zohar vol. III, 83b), as it says (Jeremiah 2:19) ״תְּיַסְּרֵךְ רָעָתֵךְ״, and it is written (Isaiah 64:6) ״וַתְּמוּגֵנוּ בְּיַד״ etc. And the one who curses awakens the judgment through the power of the curse at the time of wrath, to hasten the imposition of the adversary upon a person to do him harm." "<b>Further,</b> one must know that the accursed portion is called by the name קְלָלָה and זְעִימָה, and Scripture likewise calls them so, as it is written (Deuteronomy 28:20) ״יְשַׁלַּח HaShem etc. אֶת הַמְּאֵרָה etc. וְאֶת הַמִּגְעֶרֶת״ — and these are the names of the categories of destroyers. קְלָלָה corresponds to grave transgressions, and זַעַם to minor transgressions. Therefore, when Balaam wished to curse a nation, he would choose a time of wrath; and since all of them were objects of wrath because of their evil deeds, his words were effective against them. And Balak reasoned that the people of Israel would not be free of sin, and when his sorcerers would inform him that they had not escaped the maladies of the soul — which is the distant branch, as we have said — therefore Balaam said <b>how shall I curse</b> etc., meaning that in every branch of holiness in which the people of Israel hold fast, there is in the supernal root no dominion whatsoever for the distant portion through which he might awaken judgment upon them." "Now, there are two supreme levels to which Israel is related: the name El and the name YHWH, blessed be He. The name El — for Yaakov was called ״יִשְׂרָאֵל,״ meaning: yashar El, to exclude from the root, which is the realm of evil and is the banished portion; and the name YHWH, as it is written (Deuteronomy 32:9): ״כִּי חֵלֶק ה׳ עַמּוֹ״ — and these are the two levels of holiness that Israel attained. Therefore he said <b>מָה אֶקֹּב לֹא קַבֹּה אֵל</b>, for the root of their souls in this realm bears no trace of that evil portion, as we said above. Also, in the supernal realm — they being the roots of the souls of the righteous in the aspect of the name YHWH — <b>לֹא זָעַם ה׳</b>, meaning, even the exacting scrutiny that HaShem brings upon the righteous is not found there. And from this, what will his curses and his wrath accomplish? Now you might say that he would search in the root preceding them and in the surrounding branches — therefore he nullified this argument, for the reason that he sought and did not find, and that is his saying <b>כִּי מֵרֹאשׁ צֻרִים אֶרְאֶנּוּ</b> etc., meaning, צוּרִים are the patriarchs as is known, and he looked and saw the head of the צוּרִים, who are Terach and his fathers, and found no place from which to arouse judgments against them." "<b>And the measure</b> of the verse is as follows: the reason I say ״לֹא זָעַם״ and ״לֹא קַבֹּה״ is not that I searched in only a portion of them — that is not so; rather, I see רֹאשׁ צוּרִים, and I also searched after the root of the matriarchs, which is his saying ״וּמִגְּבָעוֹת אֲשׁוּרֶנּוּ״ — and despite all this he found no place through which to enter. And since one may raise a difficulty: how is it possible that he found no blemish among the earlier ones — Terach and his fathers — and also among Betuel and Lavan? Therefore he said <b>הֶן עָם לְבָדָד יִשְׁכֹּן וּבַגּוֹיִם לֹא יִתְחַשָּׁב</b>, meaning, לְבָדָד — that its root is not from the root of Terach and his fathers and from that branch, but rather לְבָדָד, a distinct kind, the source of Israelite souls; it has no kinship whatsoever with those who begot them. And his saying <b>וּבַגּוֹיִם לֹא יִתְחַשָּׁב</b> means that even with those who are called גּוֹיִם, who are literally their kin — namely Yishmael and Esav, both of whom are called גּוֹיִם: Yishmael, as it is written (Genesis 17:20): ״וּנְתַתִּיו לְגוֹי גָּדוֹל״; and Esav, as it is written (Genesis 25:23): ״שְׁנֵי גוֹיִם״ — they are not reckoned. So too HaShem said to Avraham (Genesis 21:12): ״כִּי בְיִצְחָק יִקָּרֵא לְךָ זֶרַע,״ to exclude Yishmael and Esav, who are not reckoned as his seed. From this point on there is no room to curse Israel, neither the righteous nor the intermediate. And although there were sins in Israel's hand — the sin of the calf and the spies and the like — HaShem had already spoken to them in the language of pardon. And perhaps for this reason HaShem was not wrathful all those days that Bilam sought to curse, heaven forbid." "<b>Further,</b> he intends in his saying <b>הֶן עָם</b> etc. to express astonishment at Balak, who wishes to curse Israel, and he said: ״הֶן עָם״ — this is the one who endures after all the nations have passed away; he stands apart, he dwells alone, while all the nations have no assurance even for many years — so how can he wish to uproot a nation that endures until the end of the world? And his saying <b>וּבַגּוֹיִם לֹא יִתְחַשָּׁב</b> means, when they are לְבָדָד they do not come under the reckoning of comparative greatness, for nothing compares to them among all the attainments the nations will reach."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:36.497294266Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}